          "text": "**Observations**\n\n- Spatial extent of the first-layer weights: the first image row (inputs 0–27) and last row (756–783) are essentially initialization-level noise (|w| < 0.04), as are columns 0–1 and 27. But row 1 (inputs 28–55) already carries clearly learned weights at columns 9–20 (e.g. 0.08–0.12 for units 1, 9, 13, 14, 15), and row 26 (inputs 728–755) carries strong weights (−0.25 to −0.29 for unit 15, +0.15 for unit 9) at columns 6–20. Horizontally, structure runs from about column 2–3 to column 25–26. So the informative region is nearly the whole 28×28 frame, not a 20×20 box with a 4-pixel blank border.\n- Character of the patterns: the receptive fields are smooth, broad blobs and vertical/horizontal bands rather than thin stroke-like or ring-like filters. Examples: unit 6 (bias +0.76) has a solid negative block in rows 3–9, columns 5–11 (upper-left \"shoulder/sleeve\" region, down to −0.31); unit 2 has a negative column stripe through the upper centre (rows 3–10, cols 8–14) and a negative block at the bottom (rows 23–25); unit 12 has a strong positive patch at rows 11–14, cols 6–9 (+0.29 to +0.35) i.e. the left mid-height edge; unit 8 is positive on the left side of rows 14–19 and negative in the centre of rows 11–13; unit 15 is positive across the top rows 3–6 and strongly negative across the bottom rows 22–26; unit 9 (bias +1.1) is positive along the top rows 0–7 and bottom-left, negative bottom-right; unit 5 is a broad positive band over the lower half (rows 18–25) of the centre; units 1/3/13 are positive over the upper-middle rows 2–6.\n- Several units show sharp sign flips in the middle of the image (e.g. unit 14 goes from +0.21 at row 10 to −0.19 at row 11 in the same columns; units 4, 7 flip sign around rows 9–12), consistent with detecting where an object's silhouette boundary sits (waist/hem/collar height, sleeve presence) rather than pen strokes.\n- Output layer: unit 6 (upper-left region empty) → class 1 strongly positive (+1.59), classes 2 and 3 strongly negative (−1.16, −1.41). Unit 15 (top filled, bottom empty) → classes 4, 6 positive, classes 7, 9 strongly negative (−1.05, −1.53). Unit 2 → classes 4, 6 positive, class 8 negative. Unit 12 (left mid-edge patch) → class 6, 0 positive, classes 1, 2, 7 negative. Class 8 has the most negative output bias (−0.73), class 7 the most positive (+0.46). Classes 0/2/4/6 tend to be grouped with the same sign in many W2 rows, and 5/7/9 likewise.\n\n**Reasoning**\n\n- MNIST digits are size-normalised into a 20×20 box centred in the 28×28 frame; pixels in rows 0–3 and 24–27 are almost always zero, so their weights never move from initialization. Here rows 1 and 26 have strongly learned weights, which rules out MNIST and similarly padded datasets (KMNIST/EMNIST are also centred with margins and their filters look stroke-like).\n- The receptive fields are large, blocky silhouette detectors (top vs. bottom filled, upper-left corner filled or empty, left mid-edge, bottom hem region) — exactly what a tiny MLP learns on clothing silhouettes that fill the frame vertically (trousers, dresses, coats) versus items confined to the middle rows (footwear) or with/without sleeves.\n- The output structure matches Fashion-MNIST's class semantics: a \"no sleeve/shoulder region\" detector votes for trouser (1) and against pullover (2) and dress (3); a \"top filled, bottom empty\" detector votes against sneaker (7) and ankle boot (9); the upper-body classes 0/2/4/6 (T-shirt, pullover, coat, shirt) and the footwear classes 5/7/9 (sandal, sneaker, boot) move together, which is the known confusion structure of Fashion-MNIST.\n\n**Dataset:** Fashion-MNIST (Zalando 28×28 grayscale clothing items).\n\n**Classes:** 0 T-shirt/top, 1 Trouser, 2 Pullover, 3 Dress, 4 Coat, 5 Sandal, 6 Shirt, 7 Sneaker, 8 Bag, 9 Ankle boot.\n\n**Confidence:** 75%",
